## Why This Is Asked

Explores bridging multi‑IdP authentication (OIDC, SAML) into a unified JWT path for a service mesh, emphasizing token binding, replay protection, PKCE, and cross‑region revocation.

## Key Concepts

- Token binding to device fingerprint
- SAML/OIDC to JWT bridging
- Token exchange (RFC 8693)
- Refresh token rotation
- Global revocation and JWKS rotation

## Code Example

```javascript
function validateToken(token, expectedAud) {
  const payload = parseJwt(token);
  if (payload.iss !== "https://idp.example.com") return false;
  if (!payload.aud.includes(expectedAud)) return false;
  if (Date.now() >= payload.exp * 1000) return false;
  return verifyJwt(token, payload.kid);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to revoke tokens across regions efficiently?
- How would you handle key rotation and JWKS dissemination?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to design a multi-tenant auth bridge that handles both OIDC and SAML inputs, normalizes to a single internal JWT, supports a token-exchange path, and enforces per-tenant policy and replay protection.

## Key Concepts
- Token validation for OIDC and SAML
- Claim mapping to an internal JWT with tenant and scopes
- Token exchange semantics for downstream services
- TTL-based per-tenant replay protection
- Middleware enforcing audience, issuer, tenant, and scope

## Code Example
```javascript
// SAML-to-claims adapter (pseudo)
function mapSamlToClaims(saml) {
  return {
    sub: saml.nameID,
    tenant: saml.attributes?.tenant,
    scope: (saml.attributes?.scopes || '').split(' ')
  };
}
```

```javascript
// Minimal internal JWT issuer (pseudo)
function issueInternalJwt(claims) {
  return jwt.sign({ sub: claims.sub, tenant: claims.tenant, scope: claims.scope, aud: 'internal-service' }, privateKey, { algorithm: 'RS256', expiresIn: '15m' });
}
```

```javascript
// Replay protection middleware (pseudo)
function replayGuard(req, res, next) {
  const jti = req.token?.jti;
  const key = req.tenant + ':' + jti;
  if (replayCache.has(key)) return res.status(401).send('replayed');
  replayCache.set(key, true, ttlMs);
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate keys with zero downtime?
- How would you test replay protection across tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests orchestration of cross-service sagas with event-sourcing, focusing on idempotency, outbox patterns, and compensating actions under partial failure.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration across Order/Payment/Inventory with CQRS
- Event contracts versioning and backward compatibility
- Outbox pattern for durable commands
- Idempotent handlers using correlationId
- Compensating actions mapped to failure modes

## Code Example
```javascript
function isDuplicate(eventId, correlationId, store) {
  const key = correlationId + ":" + eventId;
  if (store.has(key)) return true;
  store.set(key, true);
  return false;
}
```

